# Week 13 Reflection — CI/CD · Observability · Production-Readiness

One concept that stood out to me this week was CI/CD, especially the idea of using a GitHub Actions workflow to automatically run tests before code is merged into the main branch. 
I think this is a really helpful way to catch bugs early instead of finding them after the application is already deployed. 
It also helps make teamwork easier because everyone knows that the code has passed the required checks before it becomes part of the main project.

For our capstone project, I would set up a workflow that runs every time someone opens a pull request. 
The workflow would automatically run our tests and make sure everything is working correctly. 
If one of the tests fails, the deploy gate would stop the code from being merged until the issue is fixed. 
Once all the tests pass and the pull request is approved, the project can safely be merged into the main branch and deployed.

Learning about CI/CD made me realize how important automation is in software development. 
Instead of manually checking everything, the workflow helps make the process faster and more reliable. 
It also gives the team more confidence that new changes will not accidentally break existing features. 
Overall, I think using a CI/CD pipeline is a smart way to improve code quality and make the development process more organized.
